      "id": "lpic2-raid1-assembly-policy",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-2",
      "topic": "RAID1 assembly and identity",
      "unit": "raid1-assembly-policy",
      "title": "Recover a clean mirror and its saved assembly policy",
      "summary": "Identify existing RAID1 members, repair scan policy and restore the right dataset without disturbing a neighboring mirror.",
      "objective": "Restore /dev/md10 using the original reporting members /dev/vda1 and /dev/vdb1; determine their array UUID from their existing metadata. Repair and save /etc/mdadm.conf so a new targeted assembly of /dev/md10 selects both original reporting members. Keep /dev/md11 active on its original audit members /dev/vdc1 and /dev/vdd1, with correct saved assembly policy. Both active arrays must expose their original /records.txt sample data through the simulator inspection view; preserve all component data and metadata. Save or revert the editor draft, inspect the active arrays, then choose Check work.",
      "retry_guidance": "Compare saved-policy and active-identity checks separately. A manual assembly can work while saved configuration remains wrong; a saved edit does not replace a running array.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 32,
      "steps": [
        "Identify the existing member pairs",
        "Repair saved assembly policy",
        "Assemble and inspect both active identities",
        "Verify retained data and check work"
      ],
      "guide": [],
      "hints": [
        "Run mdadm --examine on both reporting members. Compare every group of Array UUID, and verify their distinct Device Roles.",
        "DEVICE lists scan candidates; the ARRAY row selects an identity for a target. A correct UUID with an omitted member is not a complete clean pair here.",
        "Use the supported targeted scan form after Save file. Compare /proc/mdstat and mdadm --detail; inspect original data as well as the presence of an array."
      ],
      "explanation": "Assembly reconnects existing matching component metadata to an active MD target; it does not create new replicas. The saved policy and active binding are independent, so both are graded. The original UUID and actual sample content distinguish reporting from audit even when both arrays look healthy.",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-201-202-objectives/",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mdadm.8.html",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/mdadm.conf.5.html",
        "https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/md.html"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Clean, synchronized two-member RAID1 with supplied abstract version-1.2 metadata and actual bounded sample replicas only. No real kernel, devices, mounted filesystem, subprocesses or host files. Literal DEVICE and UUID-based ARRAY rows; unsupported policy/commands fail clearly. Targeted scan only: real mdadm can discover arrays without ARRAY rows, but that fallback, HOMEHOST, initramfs and boot policy are outside this activity. No dirty/degraded assembly, force/create/reshape, event counters or full exam-objective coverage. The native sample-data view is simulator inspection, not a Linux command. Sources checked2026-11-08.",
